 "anatomy-steps": {
  "item/card-1": {
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Spacing",
     "question": "How dense is the layout?",
     "answer": "Spacious",
     "why": "Space lowers effort and mis-taps."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Shape",
     "question": "What should control corners say?",
     "answer": "Slightly rounded",
     "why": "Clear, slightly rounded controls."
    },
    {
     "kind": "rule",
     "situation": "items that belong together",
     "use": "space.gap.default",
     "not": "space.gap.tight",
     "why": "Items in one group sit 16px apart, not the 12px tight gap: generous space between fields stops mis-taps."
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "space.inset.container",
     "key": "default.padding",
     "value": "1.5rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "space.24",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "space.gap.group",
     "key": "default.gap",
     "value": "2rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "space.32",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "radius.element",
     "key": "radius",
     "value": "0.375rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "radius.6: We use the 6px control corner for boxes that grow in order to keep their role, as objects of one family, by inheritance from the control corner. The same radius does this by giving a text area or a list row the curve of the fields beside it. So people see one family of boxes on a form, because the control corner is slight, not a pill (shape.values), and the brand is clear (brand.q1).",
     "shares": []
    }
   ]
  },
  "item/card-2": {
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "answer",
